.TH "gdb4hpc" "1" "02-08-2021" "" ""
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
\f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] \[em] line mode parallel debugger
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
[ \f[B]--help\f[R] | \f[B]-h\f[R] ] [ \f[B]--version\f[R] | \f[B]-v\f[R]
]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
[ \f[B]--no-init\f[R] | \f[B]-n\f[R] ] [ \f[B]--silent\f[R] |
\f[B]-s\f[R] ]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
[ \f[B]--attach=JOBID\f[R] | \f[B]-j\f[R] ] [ \f[B]--exec=CMD\f[R] |
\f[B]-x\f[R] ]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
[ \f[B]--batch=FILE\f[R] | \f[B]-b\f[R] ] [ \f[B]--output=FILE\f[R] |
\f[B]-o\f[R] ]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
[ \f[B]--keep-going\f[R] | \f[B]-k\f[R] ] [ \f[B]--echo\f[R] |
\f[B]-e\f[R] ]
.SH IMPLEMENTATION
.PP
Cray Linux Environment (CLE)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] is a GDB-based parallel debugger used to debug
applications compiled with CCE, PGI, GNU and Intel Fortran, C and C++
compilers.
It allows programmers to either launch an application or attach to an
already-running application.
.PP
\f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] also includes \f[I]comparative debugger\f[R]
technology that enables programmers to compare data structures between
two executing applications.
Cray, however, recommends accessing the comparative debugger technology
through the new Cray Comparative Debugger (CCDB) with graphical user
interface (GUI) that enhances the parallel debugging capabilities of
\f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R].
For further information on CCDB, see \f[B]ccdb\f[R](1).
.SS Options
.PP
The \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] debugger accepts the following options:
.TP
\f[B]--help\f[R], \f[B]-h\f[R]
Display help text.
.TP
\f[B]--no-init\f[R], \f[B]-n\f[R]
Don\[cq]t execute any commands from the gdb4hpc_init file.
(By default, $HOME/.gdb4hpc/gdb4hpc_init)
.TP
\f[B]--silent\f[R], \f[B]-s\f[R]
Suppress version banner on startup.
.TP
\f[B]--version\f[R], \f[B]-v\f[R]
Display version.
.TP
\f[B]--attach=JOBID\f[R], \f[B]-j\f[R]
Immediately attach to application with JOBID.
.TP
\f[B]--exec=CMD\f[R], \f[B]-x\f[R]
Execute CMD at startup.
This option can be used multiple times.
Commands passed here are executed after commands in gdb4hpc_init.
.SS Batch mode options:
.TP
\f[B]--batch=FILE\f[R], \f[B]-b\f[R]
Execute debugger commands from FILE and exit.
.TP
\f[B]--output=FILE\f[R], \f[B]-o\f[R]
Redirect stdout/stderr to FILE.
Must be used with --batch.
.TP
\f[B]--keep-going\f[R], \f[B]-k\f[R]
In batch mode, don\[cq]t exit when an error is encountered.
.TP
\f[B]--echo\f[R], \f[B]-e\f[R]
In batch mode, echo each command before executing it.
.SS \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] Usage
.PP
\f[B]Procedure 1. To begin using gdb4hpc\f[R]
.PP
\f[I]Step 1\f[R].
Load the \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] module if it is not already loaded.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
% module load gdb4hpc
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]Step 2\f[R].
Compile the application using either the \f[B]-g\f[R] or
\f[B]-G\f[R]\f[I]n\f[R] option of the relevant compiler to include
additional debugger information required by \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R].
For example, if compiling the Fortran application
\f[B]myprog42.f90\f[R], enter this command:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
% ftn -g -o myapp_42 myprog42.f90
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If using the comparative debugging features with CCE, it is recommended
to add \f[B]-e0 -ez\f[R] to the \f[B]ftn\f[R] compile line or \f[B]-h
zero\f[R] to the \f[B]cc\f[R] compile line to force memory
initialization to zero.
This helps to avoid false positive differences due to comparing
uninitialized memory.
.PP
\f[I]Step 3\f[R].
For systems that normally operate in batch mode only, Cray
\f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] has three methods for interacting with the supported
WLM systems (ALPS, hybrid SLURM, and native SLURM):
.IP \[bu] 2
Interactive batch: Prior to launching \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R], request the
compute node resources required for the debugging session.
For example, if PBS Pro is the site\[aq]s batch system, enter this
command:
.RS 2
.PP
% qsub -IV -l mppwidth=w
.RE
.PP
Where \f[I]w\f[R] is the number of ranks required.
Then after initiating \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R], subsequent use of
\f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] commands will employ these resources.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[B]launch\f[R] command\[aq]s \f[B]--qsub\f[R] or
\f[B]--sbatch\f[R] options will cause a batch job to be submitted.
See \f[I]Workload Manager Support\f[R] for more information about
modifying existing job scripts to work with \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[B]session\f[R] command creates a new WLM session that will be
associated with all future \f[B]launch\f[R] commands.
See \f[I]Workload Manager Support\f[R] for more information about
modifying existing job scripts to work with \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] If using the \f[B]launch\f[R] command multiple times to
launch applications with either the interactive batch method or the
\f[B]session\f[R] command method under the ALPS reservation system, keep
the following in mind when determining the number of PEs to request -
ALPS will allocate whole increments of compute nodes to the applications
regardless of the number of PEs (\f[B]aprun -n\f[R] argument) requested.
For example, if running on a system with 32 PEs per compute node and
using process handles \f[B]$a{8}\f[R] and \f[B]$b{8}\f[R], the user
should request 64 PEs, or 8 PEs per node, using WLM arguments to ensure
enough PEs are allocated to satisfy both \f[B]launch\f[R] commands.
.PP
\f[I]Step 4\f[R].
The application\[aq]s WLM-specific application identifier is needed to
attach to an application that was previously launched outside of
\f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R].
For ALPS, this is the application process ID (\f[B]apid\f[R]); for
native SLURM, this is a combination of the job ID (\f[B]job_id\f[R]) and
the job step ID (\f[B]step_id\f[R]).
.PP
For ALPS, use the \f[B]apstat\f[R] command to determine the
application\[aq]s \f[B]apid\f[R].
For example, if the application \f[B]myapp_42\f[R] was launched using
\f[B]aprun\f[R], the following \f[B]apstat\f[R] output shows the
application\[aq]s \f[B]apid\f[R] is \f[B]651486\f[R].
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
% apstat
Compute node summary
   arch config     up    use   held  avail   down
     XT   1072   1066     24     49    993      6

No pending applications are present

Total placed applications: 3
Placed  Apid ResId     User   PEs Nodes    Age   State Command
      651486    60  user123    64     8   0h27m  run   myapp_42
      651488    61  user123    64     8   0h27m  run   app_LIB
      651490    62  user124     8     8   0h06m  run   IOR
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
For native SLURM, use the \f[B]squeue\f[R] and \f[B]sstat\f[R] commands
to obtain the \f[B]job_id\f[R] and \f[B]step_id\f[R], respectively.
The SLURM application identifier should be provided as
\f[I]job_id\f[R].\f[I]step_id\f[R].
For example, if the application \f[B]a.out\f[R] was launched using
\f[B]srun\f[R], the following \f[B]squeue\f[R] output shows the
application\[aq]s \f[B]job_id\f[R] is \f[B]22702\f[R], and the
\f[B]sstat\f[R] output shows the application\[aq]s \f[B]step_id\f[R] is
\f[B]0\f[R].
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
% squeue
             JOBID PARTITION     NAME     USER ST       TIME  NODES NODELIST(REASON)
             22702  amdMI100    a.out  user123  R      32:15      1 node010
             22701   amdMI60    faces  user124  R       4:32      1 node004
         
% sstat --format=JobId 22702
       JobID 
------------ 
22702.0      
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If the WLM is PALS or GENERIC, the \f[B]job_id\f[R] is simply the
process id of the mpiexec process.
There are two ways to find the mpiexec process id:
.PP
\f[B]Procedure 1.\f[R] Create an interactive job with \f[B]qsub -I -V
-l<resource list>\f[R] then launch your job directly via
\f[B]mpiexec\f[R].
In this case, the interactive job already puts you shell on the
launching node, so can just run \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] (step 5) and attach
using the mpiexec process id.
.PP
\f[B]Procedure 2.\f[R] If the job was launched via a batch script, use
\f[B]qstat -n\f[R] to find the launching node.
Then \f[B]ssh\f[R] to that node, find your \f[B]mpiexec\f[R] process,
run \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] (step 5) on that node and attach using the mpiexec
process id.
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] These procedures are temporary work-arounds for the
preliminary PALS support in \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]Step 5\f[R].
Use the \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] command to launch the parallel debugger.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
% gdb4hpc
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The system responds as follows:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
gdb4hpc 2.4 - Cray Line Mode Parallel Debugger 
With Cray Comparative Debugging Technology.
Copyright 2007-2014,2020 Hewlett Packard Enterprise Development LP.
Copyright 1996-2013 University of Queensland. All rights Reserved.

Type \[dq]help\[dq] for a list of commands.
Type \[dq]help <cmd>\[dq] for detailed help about a command.
dbg all>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]Step 6\f[R].
Once \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R] is launched and the \f[B]dbg all>\f[R] prompt is
displayed, the program uses a command-line interface similar to that
used by \f[B]gdb\f[R].
Enter \f[B]help\f[R] for more information about supported commands.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> help
assign          Change the value of an application or debugger variable.
attach          Attach to an application.
backtrace       Print backtrace of all stack frames.
break           Set breakpoint at specified line or function.
build           Build an assertion script.
compare         Compare the contents of two variables.
continue        Continue execution of application.
decomposition   Define a decomposition scheme.
defset          Create a set of processes.
delete          Delete a breakpoint.
disable         Disable a breakpoint.
down            Move down one or more stack frames.
enable          Enable a breakpoint.
finish          Execute application until each rank\[aq]s current function returns.
focus           Set the current process set.
frame           Print the currently selected stack frame.
gdbmode         Enter direct dgb mode (experimental).
halt            Halt execution of attached/launched application.
help            Display help information about commands.
info            Display information about the application being debugged.
kill            Kill attached/launched application.
launch          Launch an application.
list            List source for specified function or line.
maint           Commands for use by debugger maintainers.
next            Step application, proceeding through subroutine calls.
print           Print the value of an expression.
quit            Exit the debugger.
release         Release attached/launched application from debugger control.
session         Create a new WLM session.
set             Set information about the debugger environment.
show            Show information about the debugger environment.
source          Read debugger commands from a file.
start           Start executing an assertion script.
step            Step application until it reaches a different source line.
stop            Stop the currently executing assertion script.
tbreak          Set a temporary breakpoint at a specified line or function.
unset           Unset information in the debugger environment.
up              Move up one or more stack frames.
usage           Display usage information about script mode commands.
viewset         Display information about process sets.
watch           Set watchpoint on specified expression.
whatis          Print the data type of an expression.
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To view detailed information about a specific command, enter
\f[B]help\f[R] \f[I]command\f[R].
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> help break

Summary: Set breakpoint command.
Usage: break [location]
Example command: break main.c:20

Arguments: location(line number, function name, or address)

Set a breakpoint in every application rank in the current process set.
The location of the breakpoint can be defined as a line number, a function
name, or \[dq]*\[dq] and an address. If an address is specified, it must begin
with an asterix (*). For example, break *0x0000000000400693 would break
at address 0x400693.
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]Step 7\f[R].
When finished using \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R], enter \f[B]quit\f[R] to exit the
debugger.
Upon exit, launched applications are killed (execution terminated) and
attached applications are released from the debugger\[aq]s control but
are allowed to continue execution.
Applications can be killed or released from within \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R],
prior to exiting, with the \f[B]kill\f[R] or \f[B]release\f[R] commands,
respectively.
.SS Process Sets and Variables
.PP
When an application is debugged using \f[B]gdb4hpc\f[R], the application
ranks are arranged into a group called a \f[I]process set\f[R].
The default process set is named \f[I]all\f[R] and contains the ranks of
every application that is currently being debugged.
Debugger variables are used to reference process sets and are defined by
the \f[B]attach\f[R] and \f[B]launch\f[R] commands.
Debugger variables are denoted by a dollar sign (\f[B]$\f[R]) followed
by the variable name, such as \f[B]$a\f[R], to represent a scalar
variable, or \f[B]$b{1024}\f[R], with required squiggly braces, to
represent an array variable.
\f[B]$a\f[R] and \f[B]$b\f[R] are referred to as application
\f[I]handles\f[R].
.PP
A user can refer to specific application ranks and to ranks from
multiple invoked applications by using the \f[B]defset\f[R] command to
define a new process set containing the desired ranks and then
\f[B]focus\f[R] on that set.
The expression defining the process set must be a comma-separated list
of valid application handles and valid ranks.
In the following examples, assume that \f[B]$a{64}\f[R] and
\f[B]$b{64}\f[R] represent two launched applications.
.PP
\f[B]Example 1: Reference ranks from a single application\f[R]
.PP
To reference ranks 0 through 31 and 63 from \f[B]$a\f[R], define a new
process set \f[B]$c\f[R].
Note that \[dq]\f[B]..\f[R]\[dq] denotes a range of ranks.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> defset $c $a{0..31,63}
dbg all> focus $c
dbg c>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Example 2: Reference ranks from an existing process set\f[R]
.PP
To reference ranks 0 through 2 and 63 based on process set \f[B]$c\f[R]
defined in \f[I]Example 1: Reference ranks from a single
application\f[R], define a new process set \f[B]$d\f[R].
Note that \[dq]\f[B]..\f[R]\[dq] denotes a range of ranks.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> defset $d $c{0..2,32}
dbg all> focus $d
dbg d> 
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The \f[B]viewset\f[R] command can be used to view which application
ranks are in a defined process set.
After issuing the commands from \f[I]Example 1: Reference ranks from a
single application\f[R] and \f[I]Example 2: Reference ranks from an
existing process set\f[R], \f[B]viewset\f[R] shows:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg d> viewset
Name     Procs
all     a{0..63),b{0..63}
a       a{0..63}
b       b{0..63}
c       a{0..31,63}
d       a{0..2,63}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Example 3: Reference ranks from multiple applications\f[R]
.PP
To reference ranks 0 through 31 and 63 from \f[B]$a\f[R] and ranks 31
and 63 from \f[B]$b\f[R], define a new process set \f[B]$e\f[R].
Note that \[dq]\f[B]..\f[R]\[dq] denotes a range of ranks.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> defset $e $a{0..31,63},$b{31,63}
dbg all> focus $e
dbg e>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Example 4: Focus on a temporary process set\f[R]
.PP
By using the \f[B]focus\f[R] command, a user can focus explicitly on a
subset of ranks without defining a new process set.
\f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R] creates a temporary process set that can subsequently
be used in process targeted commands.
The temporary process set is removed when the focus is changed.
Unlike defined process sets, a temporary process set can only contain
ranks from one application.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> focus $b{0,2,5..8,31}
dbg b_temp> bt
b{0,2,5..8,31}: #0 0x00000000004010ff in main at /tmp/usr/test/c_test.c:132
dbg b_temp> focus $all
dbg all>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Finally, a user can refer to every rank of an application by focusing on
the process handle that was specified when the application was first
attached or launched.
When a user focuses on a process set, it becomes the \f[I]current
set\f[R].
.SS Application Variables
.PP
Application variables are referenced as follows:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
[$proc_set::]var
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Where $\f[I]proc_set\f[R] is an optional process set identifier, and
\f[I]var\f[R] is the desired application variable.
If no process set is declared, the executing command will act on all
ranks in the current set.
To reference variable \f[B]max\f[R] in all ranks of the process set
\f[B]$b\f[R], use:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$b::max
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To reference a rank-specific element, use:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$b{4}::max
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To reference multiple rank-specific elements, use:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$b{0..3,12}::max
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Note that \[dq]\f[B]..\f[R]\[dq] denotes a range of ranks.
.PP
To reference application variable arrays, use the application\[aq]s
coding language syntax.
For example, to reference elements of the array \f[B]xval\f[R] in rank 2
of process set $b, use:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
In Fortran - $b{2}::xval(3,2)
In c - $b{2}::xval[2][3]
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Launching an Application
.PP
The \f[B]launch\f[R] command transparently invokes the WLM job launch
command (i.e., \f[B]aprun\f[R] or \f[B]srun\f[R]) to launch an
application.
Following the launch, the application is held immediately before
execution is passed to its \f[B]MPI_init()\f[R], \f[B]shmem_init()\f[R],
or \f[B]main()\f[R] routine and is ready for further \f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R]
commands.
Integration with \f[B]qsub\f[R] and \f[B]sbatch\f[R] is also supported
as described in \f[I]Workload Manager Support\f[R].
Launching on systems whose default workload manager is not SLURM or
ALPS, or launching an application using a non default launcher requires
passwordless (public key) ssh to the compute nodes of active jobs.
Contact a system administrator or the system usage guide for details on
whether this is supported and how to set this up.
.PP
The simplest form of the \f[B]launch\f[R] command is:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
launch $proc_set application
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
$\f[I]proc_set\f[R] is a single debugger variable array that specifies
the number of application ranks to be launched.
For example, the following will launch the application \f[B]tstapp\f[R]
via the WLM job launcher with 1024 ranks (\f[B]-n1024\f[R]) assigned to
the process set \f[B]$b\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> launch $b{1024} tstapp
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The \f[B]launch\f[R] command also accepts the following optional
arguments.
Option values must be enclosed within quotation marks, such as
\[dq]args.\[dq]
.TP
\f[B]--launcher=\f[R]\[dq]\f[I]launcher_name\f[R]\[dq] | \f[B]-l\f[R] \[lq]\f[I]launcher_name\f[R]\[rq]
Launches the job with the launcher specified by \f[I]launcher_name\f[R].
This option is not commonly needed and should be used with care.
This option specifies the name of the application launcher executable as
either an absolute path or the name of an executable reachable in PATH.
Setting this option only affects the name of the launcher executable
itself and does not change the launch system.
To launch with a non default launcher that is not related to the default
running workload manager (such as mpiexec.hydra on a slurm system), this
variable should be using in conjunction with setting CRAY_CTI_WLM to
\[dq]generic\[dq] to ensure the launch mechanisms work with the
specified launcher.
.TP
\f[B]--launcher-args=\f[R] \[dq]\f[I]launcher_args\f[R]\[dq] | \f[B]-g\f[R] \[dq]\f[I]launcher_args\f[R]\[dq]
Passes \f[I]launcher_args\f[R] to the WLM job launch command.
.TP
\f[B]--launcher-input=\f[R] \[dq]\f[I]input_file\f[R]\[dq] | \f[B]-i\f[R] \[dq]\f[I]input_file\f[R]\[dq]
Redirects the \f[B]stdin\f[R] of the WLM job launch command to be
\f[I]input_file\f[R].
This is useful for applications requiring input from \f[B]stdin\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]--args=\f[R] \[dq]\f[I]app_args\f[R]\[dq] | \f[B]-a\f[R] \[dq]\f[I]app_args\f[R]\[dq]
Passes \f[I]app_args\f[R] to the application executable.
.TP
\f[B]--env=\f[R]\[dq]\f[I]name\f[R]=\f[I]value\f[R]\[dq],\f[B]--env=\f[R]\[dq]\f[I]name\f[R]=\f[I]value\f[R]\[dq],\&...
Sets the environment variable (defined by \f[I]name\f[R]) to
\f[I]value\f[R], for this job session instance.
Note that \f[B]--env=\f[R] can be used more than once to set multiple
environment variables.
.TP
\f[B]--gpu\f[R]
Enables the use of an OpenACC supported version of \f[B]gdb\f[R] for
debugging on a Nvidia CUDA 5.5 or above GPU.
Sets several environment variables for debugging.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] OpenACC debugging is only supported through the
\f[B]launch\f[R] command.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]--qsub=\f[R]\[dq]\f[I]batch_template\f[R]\[dq]
Submits \f[I]batch_template\f[R] as a PBS job from which an application
will launch for debugging.
.TP
\f[B]--sbatch=\f[R]\[dq]\f[I]batch_template\f[R]\[dq]
Submits \f[I]batch_template\f[R] as a SLURM job from which an
application will launch for debugging.
.TP
\f[B]--workdir=\f[R] \[dq]\f[I]work_path\f[R]\[dq] | \f[B]-d\f[R] \[dq]\f[I]work_path\f[R]\[dq]
Changes the current working directory, relative to its present setting
where \f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R] was invoked, to \f[I]work_path\f[R].
This is useful for applications that write files to the current working
directory.
By default, if the \f[B]--workdir=\f[R] option is specified without a
path, the current working directory will be changed to the location of
the application\[aq]s executable file.
.SS Caveat for Launching a Serial Application
.PP
Parallel applications contain a communication mechanism that causes a
startup barrier code to be pulled in during compilation.
\f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R] uses the barrier code to hold the application at its
entry point in preparation to begin the debugging process.
Typically, serial applications do not have a barrier code and,
therefore, must be modified in order to be debugged using
\f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R].
.PP
Users can add either an \f[B]MPI_Init()\f[R] call or a fake startup
barrier at the beginning of their code.
For example, using C code:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
int dummyBarrier = 1;
while (dummyBarrier);
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
After attaching to the application in \f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R], issue the
following command and proceed to debug as usual:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> assign dummyBarrier 0
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If the fake startup barrier method is utilized, the user must use the
\f[B]attach\f[R] command; the \f[B]launch\f[R] is unavailable and will
hang indefinitely.
.SS Attaching to a Previously Launched Application
.PP
Use the \f[B]attach\f[R] command to attach to an executing application
that was launched outside of \f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R].
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dgb all> attach $proc_set app_ident
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Where $\f[I]proc_set\f[R] is the debugger variable to be associated with
the application, and \f[I]app_ident\f[R] is the WLM-specific application
identifier of the running application.
$\f[I]proc_set\f[R] \f[I]must not\f[R] include array syntax; the size of
the $\f[I]proc_set\f[R] array is automatically set to the number of
ranks in the job.
For ALPS, \f[I]app_ident\f[R] is the application process ID
(\f[B]apid\f[R]); for native SLURM on Cray XC systems,
\f[I]app_ident\f[R] is a combination of the job ID (\f[B]job_id\f[R])
and the job step ID (\f[B]step_id\f[R]) and must be provided as
\f[I]job_id\f[R].\f[I]step_id\f[R].
For Cray clusters and all other systems, app_ident should be the pid of
the launcher process that started the application.
This can be obtained using ps.
.PP
The following command instructs \f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R] to associate the
debugger variable \f[B]$tst42\f[R] with the application that has
\f[B]apid\f[R] 651486.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> attach $tst42 651486
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Workload Manager Support
.PP
Two methods exist for submitting batch jobs directly to a WLM:
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[B]launch\f[R] command\[aq]s \f[B]--qsub\f[R] or
\f[B]--sbatch\f[R] options can be used to submit a PBS or SLURM job,
respectively, from within which an application will be launched for
debugging.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[B]session\f[R] command will create a new WLM session to which all
future \f[B]launch\f[R] or \f[B]attach\f[R] commands will be associated.
This allows multiple \f[B]launch\f[R] commands to be used in the same
batch session.
\f[B]session\f[R] accepts the same \f[B]--qsub\f[R] or
\f[B]--sbatch\f[R] options as \f[B]launch\f[R].
.PP
For either method, the existing application job script is first modified
by placing the comments \f[B]#cray_debug_start\f[R] and
\f[B]#cray_debug_end\f[R] around the job launch command (i.e.,
\f[B]aprun\f[R] or \f[B]srun\f[R]) that is to be debugged.
Everything between the comment lines will be ignored; the original WLM
job launch command must be recreated through \f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R]\[aq]s
\f[B]launch\f[R] command arguments.
For example, the job script \f[B]sample.pbs\f[R] is modified with the
\f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R] comments, for ALPS:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
#cray_debug_start
aprun -n128 -N32 a.out
#cray_debug_end
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
For SLURM:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
#cray_debug_start
srun -n128 -ntasks-per-node=32 a.out
#cray_debug_end
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The following \f[B]launch\f[R] command will then submit
\f[B]sample.pbs\f[R] as a PBS job from within which the application
\f[B]a.out\f[R] will be launched:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dgb all> launch $a{128} --launcher-args=\[dq]-N32\[dq] --qsub=sample.pbs a.out
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Using the \f[B]session\f[R] command, this would be accomplished as
follows:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dgb all> session --qsub=sample.pbs
dgb all> launch $a{128} --launcher-args=\[dq]-N32\[dq] a.out
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] If either the \f[B]launch\f[R] or \f[B]session\f[R]
command is interrupted before completion, the job reservation must be
manually deleted from the queueing system.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.TP
\f[B]CRAY_APRUN_PATH\f[R]
Defines the location of the Cray-provided \f[B]aprun\f[R] binary.
Used if the \f[B]aprun\f[R] binary has been renamed or moved from its
default location.
.RS
.PP
Default: not defined
.RE
.TP
\f[B]GDB4HPC_CFG_DIRECTORY\f[R]
Defines the general gdb4hpc configuration directory.
This directory is where the gdb4hpc_init file is read from, and where
the history file is stored by default.
.RS
.PP
Default: $HOME/.gdb4hpc
.RE
.TP
\f[B]GDB4HPC_HIST_DIR\f[R]
Defines the directory into which the \f[B].gbd4hpc_history\f[R] file is
located.
.RS
.PP
Default: not set.
If not set, GDB4HPC_CFG_DIRECTORY will be used.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]GDB4HPC_PMI_INIT_SLEEP\f[R]
Sets extra time (in seconds) to sleep after finding the
\f[B]pmi_attribs\f[R] file during early attach.
This should only be set if \f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R] is attaching to the
application during the \f[B]pmi_init\f[R] function and causing the
debugger to hang.
.RS
.PP
Default: not set
.RE
.TP
\f[B]GDB4HPC_STARTUP_TIMEOUT\f[R]
Sets the MRNet startup timeout in seconds.
.RS
.PP
Default: not set
.RE
.TP
\f[B]CRAY_CTI_WLM\f[R]
Sets the workload manager that gbd4hpc will use to launch or attach to
an application.
Accepts one of three values: \[dq]alps\[dq], \[dq]slurm\[dq], or
\[dq]generic\[dq].
\[dq]generic\[dq] uses a more portable launch system that can be used on
many types of systems and application launchers but can be less
performant.
Using \[dq]generic\[dq] requires setting up paswordless ssh to the
compute nodes as it uses ssh to launch the debuggers.
Use of \[dq]generic\[dq] also requires CRAY_CTI_LAUNCHER_NAME to be set
to the name of the application launcher to use.
.RS
.PP
Default: not set
.RE
.TP
\f[B]CRAY_CTI_LAUNCHER_NAME\f[R]
Sets the launcher executable to use for application launch.
This can be the name of an executable reachable in PATH or an absolute
path.
Setting this option only affects the name of the launcher executable
itself and does not change the launch system.
To launch with a non default launcher that is not related to the default
running workload manager (such as mpiexec.hydra on a slurm system), this
variable should be used in conjunction with setting CRAY_CTI_WLM to
\[dq]generic\[dq] to ensure the launch mechanisms work with the
specified launcher.
.RS
.PP
Default: not set
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
A demo directory with sample codes is included in the \f[B]gbd4hpc\f[R]
release.
The following example uses the executable created by entering the
command: \f[B]make hello_mpi_c\f[R].
.PP
Launch one instance of \f[B]hello_mpi_c\f[R] with 8 ranks.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> launch $tst{8} hello_mpi_c
Starting alps application, please wait...
Creating MRNet communication network...
Waiting for debug servers to attach to MRNet communications network...
Timeout in 60 seconds. Please wait for the attach to complete.
Number of dbgsrvs connected: [1];  Timeout Counter: [0]
Number of dbgsrvs connected: [1];  Timeout Counter: [1]
Number of dbgsrvs connected: [8];  Timeout Counter: [0]
Finalizing setup...
Launch complete.
tst{0..7}: Initial breakpoint, main at /lus/nid/user/tests/hello_mpi.c:16
dbg all>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Set a breakpoint at line 21 of source file \f[B]hello_mpi.c\f[R] and
continue execution.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> break hello_mpi.c:21
tst{0..7}: Breakpoint 1: file hello_mpi.c, line 21.
dbg all> continue
tst{0..7}: Breakpoint 1, main at hello_mpi.c
dbg all>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Print the value of myRank for each rank in the current process set.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dbg all> print myRank
tst{0}: 0
tst{1}: 1
tst{2}: 2
tst{3}: 3
tst{4}: 4
tst{5}: 5
tst{6}: 6
tst{7}: 7
\f[R]
.fi
